# unifi-otel — Docker Compose environment
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# then edit .env. Compose reads .env automatically; it is not read by the
# collector directly, so a variable that is not listed in the service's
# `environment:` block in docker-compose.yml will not reach it.
#
# No credentials appear in this file, and none are needed. The default
# config set is logs only — SNMP polling is the only part of this project
# that needs a secret, it is off by default, and it is not wired into
# docker-compose.yml at all. See docs/snmp.md.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED — timezone
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The IANA timezone of your UniFi gateway, e.g. Europe/London,
# America/New_York, Australia/Sydney. Match the gateway, not the machine
# running Docker, if the two differ.
#
# This is shipped EMPTY on purpose. Compose will refuse to start until
# you set it, because the failure mode if you get it wrong is silent:
#
#   - The gateway stamps RFC3164 syslog in LOCAL time with no offset.
#   - The receiver has no way to know what that offset was, so it is told
#     here.
#   - If it is empty, the receiver falls back to UTC without logging
#     anything. Timestamps then land a whole number of hours out and
#     everything else about the record looks perfectly fine.
#
# CEF records are immune — they are re-stamped from the CEF UNIFIutcTime
# field, which is real UTC. So a half-broken install where the SIEM
# records are on time and the device records are not is exactly this.
#
# To check: docs/quickstart.md has the detection recipe.
UNIFI_SYSLOG_TIMEZONE=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED for `docker compose up` — where to send the parsed records
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# host:port of anything that speaks OTLP/gRPC: your own gateway
# collector, or a backend that terminates OTLP itself. TLS is off by
# default (see collector/40-exporters.yaml).
#
# NOT needed for `docker compose up -d collector-debug`, which prints to
# stdout and exports nowhere. Leave this empty until you have a backend.
#
# If you leave it empty and run the export path anyway, the collector
# exits at startup with:
#
#   Error: exporters::otlp_grpc/gateway: requires a non-empty "endpoint"
#
# That is deliberate — the config's built-in default is a Kubernetes
# in-cluster DNS name that cannot resolve under Compose, and a hard stop
# beats a container that looks healthy and drops everything.
#
# See docs/destinations.md for what to put at the far end.
OTLP_GATEWAY_ENDPOINT=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL — host ports
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The receivers bind 5514/udp and 6601/tcp INSIDE the container so it can
# run as non-root; these are the host ports those are published on. The
# defaults are the standard syslog ports, which is what a UniFi gateway
# expects to send to.
#
# Change them if something already owns 514/601 on this host, or if you
# are on rootless Docker and cannot bind below 1024. If you do, set the
# port explicitly on the UniFi side to match.
#UNIFI_SYSLOG_UDP_PORT=514
#UNIFI_SYSLOG_TCP_PORT=601

# The health_check extension, published on loopback only. Probe it with
#   curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:13133
#UNIFI_HEALTH_PORT=13133
